Same work (encode the Base64 text of 500,000 bytes of "a" per run) and the same library-against-library shape as the reference: the reference calls the base64-simd crate (SIMD), this calls the language's `base64` builtin, which uses AVX2 where the CPU has it and a scalar loop elsewhere (runtime/mito.c; checked against a Python reference on 1,100+ cases in tests/simd_base64). Each run copies the bytes into the request's text area (`text_from`) or the encoded field into it, which the reference does not; the first characters the final message needs are kept in a field. main_loop.mito is the earlier version, a plain scalar loop written in the language (1.27 s and 1.03 s), kept for comparison.
